 "drivingQuestion": "Why was the Yom Kippur pan long-handled?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "so the High Priest's arm can support it",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "yoma.44b.15",
   "daf": "44b",
   "sentenceId": 14,
   "he": "בְּכׇל יוֹם הָיְתָה כְּבֵידָה, וְהַיּוֹם קַלָּה. תָּנָא, בְּכׇל יוֹם הָיְתָה גִּלְדָּהּ עָבֶה, וְהַיּוֹם רַךְ. בְּכׇל יוֹם הָיְתָה קְצָרָה, וְהַיּוֹם אֲרוּכָּה. מַאי טַעְמָא — כְּדֵי שֶׁתְּהֵא זְרוֹעוֹ שֶׁל כֹּהֵן גָּדוֹל מְסַיַּיעְתּוֹ.",
   "en": "§ The mishna states: «On every» other «day,» the coal pan «was heavy, but» on «this day» it was «light. It was taught» in a baraita: «On every» other «day its side was thick but» on «this day» it was «soft» and thin. «On every» other «day» its handle «was short but» on «this day it was long. What is the reason? So that the arm of the High Priest could assist him» in carrying the coal pan, i.e., he could support the coal pan by resting it against his arm rather than bear the entire weight in his hand."
  }
 ],
